1
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:08,320
Hello everyone, and thank
you for joining my session.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,180
My name is Yuki.

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:14,900
Today, we're diving into looking
at how and why your Swift code

4
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:16,000
works in your app.

5
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:23,680
Here's today's agenda. First, I'll
talk about why it's worth going deeper

6
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:24,700
into Swift.

7
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:30,400
Next, we'll look at how your Swift
program runs on each platform, from

8
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,400
the perspective of the Runtime.

9
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,180
and we'll see how the compiler
handles your Swift code safely.

10
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:43,130
And finally, I'll share some practical
tips to help you become friends

11
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:46,940
with Swift. Let's start with the motivation.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,660
Where should we go deeper into Swift?

13
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:53,800
As you know, Swift is now
a multi-platform language.

14
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:57,920
Every day, we build apps for
Apple platforms with Swift.

15
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:00,380
But Swift doesn't stop there.

16
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,920
It also runs beyond Apple platforms.

17
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:09,540
They're still experimental, but Swift
is coming to embedded systems, and even

18
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:10,120
Android.

19
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:15,780
With Swift reaching all these
platforms, it's a great time to look at

20
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:17,440
what's going on under the hood.

21
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:21,920
So, why should we go deeper into Swift?

22
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:28,040
First, Swift is growing into a true
multi-platform language, as we just saw.

23
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:33,000
Second, a platform-native development really
benefits from knowing what's underneath.

24
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:38,980
And finally, it shapes your
developer experience in meaningful

25
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:39,540
ways.

26
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,020
As an example, let's look at API availability.

27
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:48,900
This one feels less like a benefit
and more like a frustration, until

28
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:50,900
you understand what's happening underneath.

29
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:55,735
I'm sure many of you have hit this wall.

30
00:01:55,775 --> 00:01:59,220
Your app's deployment target
caps which APIs you can call.

31
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:05,560
And without the available keyword,
code like this simply won't compile.

32
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:12,760
For example, let's say we have this code using
@Observable, and we're building for iOS 15.

33
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:19,820
Then we get this error, telling us that
@Observable isn't available on our target OS.

34
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,820
So we need to mark it with available iOS 17,

35
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:27,060
to get this code to compile.

36
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:35,500
And there's one more: our Swift
code comes with invisible costs.

37
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,500
As an example, let's take
ARC—Automatic Reference Counting.

38
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:44,860
At compile time, the compiler inserts
extra code into yours to manage reference

39
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:45,500
counts.

40
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:49,080
And it's not just ARC.

41
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:53,540
All kinds of code instructions
get injected at compile time.

42
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:02,220
So, look beyond the code you write,
and understand how the compiler really

43
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:03,120
handles it.

44
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:06,560
That's a big step toward better performance.

45
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:10,860
For example, take the code on the left.

46
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:15,000
At compile time, code like the
one on the right gets inserted.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
These are the calls that manage reference
counts.

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,970
When you're writing everyday app code,

49
00:03:22,970 --> 00:03:23,300
you'd never notice them.

50
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:28,460
But in performance-critical code,
they can become a real problem.

51
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:35,220
These problems, and the reasons behind
them, are invisible when you're looking at

52
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:38,660
your everyday Swift code.

53
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,460
To see them, we need to dig
into what Swift really is.

54
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,160
And that's exactly what we do today.

55
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:51,060
I’ll give you the background, and the
tools to face these problems head-on.

56
00:03:57,020 --> 00:04:00,480
Now, let's begin our first journey
into the mechanism of Swift:
the Runtime.

57
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:04,760
We'll see how it works, and the
benefits it brings along the way.

58
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,740
Imagine the Swift app you work on every day.

59
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:15,120
Your app gets installed on an
Apple platform, and it runs

60
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:20,440
freely on top of the OS. It
all feels natural, right?

61
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:25,800
But there's a secret
hiding in this simple flow.

62
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:27,260
This is where Swift Runtime comes in.

63
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:31,220
It ships with the OS, and it
plays the vital role in making

64
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,000
your Swift app run.

65
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,460
So, what is the Runtime?

66
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:43,220
It's a program that supports how
your Swift code behaves at runtime.

67
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:47,540
In short, it's the heart of Swift.

68
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:48,200
It's what makes Swift feel like Swift.

69
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,820
That might sound abstract,
so let's make it concrete.

70
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:57,040
Here, it helps to clarify the roles of the

71
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:01,700
compiler and the Runtime. It makes
the whole picture much easier.

72
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,420
Think of it this way.

73
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:09,100
The compiler paves a safe road with
signals and signs, showing how your

74
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:13,660
code should proceed. And the Runtime is
the driver, actually driving the truck on

75
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:19,020
that road. Let's start with the relationship
between your app and the Runtime.

76
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:23,860
The compiled Swift code in your app
calls into the Runtime, and that's

77
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:25,280
what keeps your program running.

78
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:32,920
So, what exactly is your code
calling from the Runtime?

79
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:35,070
Let's take a look.

80
00:05:35,110 --> 00:05:40,020
When your Swift code creates an instance,
it calls the Runtime to allocate

81
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:45,640
memory. Retaining and releasing reference
counts goes through swift_retain

82
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:49,160
and swift_release instructions. Casts like as?

83
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,840
and is also rely deeply on the Runtime.

84
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:54,840
And your async code?

85
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:59,600
That's actually executed
by the Concurrency runtime.

86
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:03,200
So, the Runtime is always
working behind your code.

87
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:08,320
The Runtime has evolved over time.

88
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:14,200
Before iOS 12.2, every app
bundled its own copy of Runtime.

89
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:17,520
And that came at a cost.

90
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:20,480
Each app got larger, just to carry it.

91
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:27,520
Starting with iOS 12.2, the OS
ships the Swift Runtime as a dynamic

92
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,720
library. As a result, our apps got smaller.

93
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:35,180
Of course, there's a trade-off.

94
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:39,860
A newer OS ships a newer
Runtime, so your app can call the

95
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:41,000
newer APIs.

96
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:45,240
But an older OS still
carries an older Runtime.

97
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,260
There, the newer APIs simply don't exist.

98
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:53,660
And this is exactly why we need
the available keyword, like we saw

99
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,840
at the beginning.

100
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:59,580
Let's take a little detour here.

101
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:03,000
Earlier, I said the Runtime
ships as a dynamic library.

102
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,960
But actually, there are
two kinds of libraries.

103
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:08,260
Static libraries, and dynamic libraries.

104
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:13,600
A static library gets baked into
your app binary, and ships as one

105
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:14,540
piece with your app.

106
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:20,480
A dynamic library stays separate,
and gets loaded at runtime.

107
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:27,680
And the Swift Runtime is exactly this:
a dynamic library owned by the OS,

108
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:33,140
loaded when your app launches. The
Swift Runtime is available dynamically,

109
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:38,180
Dynamic, but whether you create static or
dynamic library in your library creation,

110
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:39,460
depends on your case.

111
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:44,380
But as I mentioned, a dynamic
library gets loaded at launch.

112
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:47,560
And that adds a cost to your launch time.

113
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:51,640
To tackle this, Apple
introduced mergeable libraries.

114
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:56,200
For the details, check out the
session Meet Mergeable Libraries from

115
00:07:56,240 --> 00:08:02,120
WWDC23. Now, let's get back on track.

116
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:07,680
Here's the thing. If the Runtime
comes from the OS, what happens when

117
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:10,020
your app is built with a
different Swift version?

118
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,560
In the past, this was a real problem.

119
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:18,540
Without a stable ABI, a mismatched
Swift version simply couldn't load

120
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:20,380
the Runtime.

121
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:25,280
This is where ABI Stability comes in.

122
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:30,900
Achieved in Swift 5, it lets apps
built with different Swift versions

123
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:32,420
all share the same Runtime.

124
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:38,460
So, what exactly is ABI Stability?

125
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:44,040
ABI stands for Application Binary Interface.

126
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:48,853
It's the contract between Swift binaries
and the Runtime, and it's now set in stone.

127
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:55,180
Thanks to this, a newer Runtime
can always load your app.

128
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:58,000
No matter which version is.

129
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,940
And there's one more piece.

130
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:08,640
Module Stability, introduced in Swift 5.1.

131
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,380
While ABI Stability works at runtime,

132
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:14,600
this one works at build time.

133
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:20,300
It lets you import a framework
built with a different compiler version.

134
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,980
For example, let's go back to

135
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:28,000
the days before Swift 5.1,

136
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,280
and see what happens when we import a framework

137
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,320
across different compiler versions.

138
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:38,840
in this case, we get an error,

139
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:41,000
and the import fails.

140
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,780
The swiftmodule file acts as
the interface of the framework.

141
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:49,160
The problem is, its format is
tied to each compiler version.

142
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:53,540
So compiler from a different
version simply can't read it.

143
00:09:55,380 --> 00:10:00,032
After Module Stability arrived, the
swiftmodule still works as before, as long as

144
00:10:00,072 --> 00:10:01,640
the compiler versions match.

145
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:05,940
But here is a new part.

146
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:11,500
When you build your library with
BUILD_LIBRARY_FOR_DISTRIBUTION set to YES, it also

147
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:13,280
emits a swiftinterface file.

148
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:17,720
And this one is readable across
the different compiler versions.

149
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:19,380
That's your stable interface.

150
00:10:22,460 --> 00:10:27,560
So, if you're maintaining a framework
or library, does this mean you can

151
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:31,380
leave it untouched, and head off on vacation?

152
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:36,920
Yes, stability keeps your old binaries

153
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:41,624
working. But it's a safety net, not a goal.

154
00:10:41,664 --> 00:10:46,660
If you're on the side of shipping
libraries, keep your code base and

155
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:48,240
your binaries up to date.

156
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:55,420
Finally, let's look at how the Runtime
works across other operating systems.

157
00:10:55,900 --> 00:11:00,500
First up Linux. On Linux, you get a choice.

158
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:05,900
You can link the Runtime statically,
or load it as dynamic library.

159
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:10,000
Next up is Windows.

160
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:15,000
Here, dynamic linking is the
mainly supported path today.

161
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:23,940
Next is Wasm. Here, libswiftCore.a
gets baked right into

162
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:29,590
the Wasm module, and that's
how Swift runs on Wasm.

163
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:36,300
And to talk to the JavaScript hosts like
Node.js, it goes through JavaScriptKit.

164
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:43,100
For the details, check out the session "Introduction
to WebAssembly for Swift Developers",

165
00:11:43,140 --> 00:11:45,000
from SwiftLeeds 2024.

166
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,080
Next up is Android.

167
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:54,520
Your Swift code, along with the
Runtime, gets built in a shared library

168
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:59,960
inside the APK. And it talks
to Java and Kotlin through

169
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,640
swift-java, using JNI.
And last, Embedded Swift.

170
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:10,700
On the left, this is what Swift
has looked like so far, with

171
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,340
Runtime sitting alongside your code.

172
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:19,568
With Embedded Swift, a slimmed-down set
of Swift features gets embedded right

173
00:12:19,608 --> 00:12:23,980
into your code. There's no
type metadata, for example.

174
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:30,090
And in exchange for being this
lightweight, it becomes a subset of the

175
00:12:30,130 --> 00:12:34,660
Swift language. For example,
take a look at this table.

176
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:40,840
Without type metadata, features like
Reflection are simply unavailable in embedded Swift.

177
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:51,000
But Embedded Swift is evolving fast.
As announced at WWDC26, the subset expanded.

178
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
Existentials and untyped throws
are now available too.

179
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:04,280
Now, for our last stop, let's look
at what the compiler really does.

180
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:11,100
Earlier, I said Runtime is a driver,
and the compiler paves the road.

181
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:15,980
So, how exactly does the compiler
pave a safe road for our code?

182
00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:22,380
To answer that, we need to see how
our code actually gets compiled,

183
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:27,420
step by step. When Swift code enters the compiler

184
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:31,360
the Parser turns it into a
structure that's easy to analyze, called

185
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:35,440
the Abstract Syntax Tree(AST).

186
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:39,140
Then, a module called Sema
fills in the type information.

187
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:45,620
Next, SILGen lowers it into an
intermediate representation called SIL.

188
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:48,640
This happens in two steps.

189
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:54,395
First raw SIL, and then canonical
SIL, with the first optimizations

190
00:13:54,435 --> 00:13:55,120
applied.

191
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:59,520
From there, it becomes LLVM IR,

192
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:05,500
then object files, and finally,
the linker puts everything together

193
00:14:05,540 --> 00:14:07,200
into your binary.

194
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:16,820
Out of all these phases, let's
zoom in on type checking.

195
00:14:18,140 --> 00:14:22,467
Sometimes, type checking comes with a cost.

196
00:14:22,507 --> 00:14:27,620
Code that relies heavily on type
inference can slow the compiler down.

197
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:35,620
And that means there's room for you to optimize.

198
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:39,620
Here is an example, a chain
of closures like this.

199
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:44,120
When I compile it on my
machine, I get this error.

200
00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:50,320
The compiler is unable to type-check
this expression in reasonable time.

201
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:55,987
In other words, type inference hits
its time limit before it can finish.

202
00:14:56,027 --> 00:15:02,072
Code like this needs a helping hand, so
type inference doesn't take too long.

203
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:06,220
type annotation helps a little.

204
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:10,000
But breaking up the expression?

205
00:15:10,040 --> 00:15:12,120
That helps a lot.

206
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,640
Some code fails with an error like this.

207
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:19,000
But that's not always the case.

208
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:25,200
Even without an error, your
build can quietly get slower.

209
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:27,620
To hunt down these hot spots,

210
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:31,120
pass these flags at build time.

211
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:35,520
The compiler will then warn you
about code that's slow to type-check.

212
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:37,620
Give them a try.

213
00:15:51,980 --> 00:15:56,880
Next, let's move on to SIL,
and what it can show us.

214
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:00,440
SIL stands for Swift Intermediate Language.

215
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:06,340
It's the intermediate language
that sits right before LLVM.

216
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:11,940
It understands the Swift
semantics, and that lets

217
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:17,000
Swift-specific optimizations run here.

218
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:20,619
This is where the SIL Optimizer comes in.

219
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:27,265
It is on between SILGen and IRGen,
so everything happens before we reach

220
00:16:27,305 --> 00:16:27,840
LLVM.

221
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:33,160
And it doesn't just run optimizations.

222
00:16:33,160 --> 00:16:38,640
It also runs diagnostics, catching problems at compile time

223
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:40,640
before your code ever runs.

224
00:16:42,920 --> 00:16:50,120
And as you can see, the SIL Optimizer
runs a whole lot of optimizations.

225
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:54,320
And many of these are unique to Swift and SIL.

226
00:16:55,220 --> 00:16:59,220
So now, let's take this greet function

227
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:01,420
and actually lower it into SIL.

228
00:17:01,420 --> 00:17:05,460
What will we find there?

229
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:13,120
First, let's look at the raw SIL.

230
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:16,440
This is the SIL before any
optimization runs

231
00:17:16,480 --> 00:17:18,620
so it's a bit hard on the eyes.

232
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:25,320
Now, let's look at the canonical
SIL, where some optimization has been applied.

233
00:17:25,620 --> 00:17:28,820
There's a lot going on here.

234
00:17:28,820 --> 00:17:35,620
But what stands out are these two,
strong_retain, and strong_release.

235
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:39,020
These are the reference
counting operations of ARC.

236
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:43,171
So at the SIL level, you can already
see the extra code the compiler

237
00:17:43,171 --> 00:17:45,200
inserts for you, ARC included.

238
00:17:46,460 --> 00:17:52,240
Depending on the context, the
optimizer removes redundant ARC

239
00:17:52,240 --> 00:17:53,040
pairs for you.

240
00:17:53,860 --> 00:17:59,360
And this is done by optimizer
passes like SemanticARCOpts and

241
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:01,980
ARCSequenceOpts.

242
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:06,820
But, don't over-trust the optimizer.

243
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:09,310
These optimizations are best-effort

244
00:18:09,310 --> 00:18:12,020
and the results can change from one compiler

245
00:18:12,020 --> 00:18:13,498
version to the next.

246
00:18:13,498 --> 00:18:18,976
So, express your performance intent
in your source code, and then

247
00:18:18,976 --> 00:18:22,500
inspect what actually comes out.

248
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:26,320
From here, let's focus on ARC.

249
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:28,120
Because ARC also has a cost.

250
00:18:28,820 --> 00:18:30,620
It's invisible in your code.

251
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:34,220
And it impacts performance.

252
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:37,820
Take this simple Swift function.

253
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:39,520
And here's its SIL.

254
00:18:39,900 --> 00:18:45,340
Even with optimizations turned on, the
retain and release calls are still there.

255
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:49,780
For example, one tool we
have here is Ownership.

256
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:57,540
With the consuming keyword, you can
move ownership to the callee. Once

257
00:18:57,580 --> 00:19:01,520
you do that, this strong
retain simply disappears.

258
00:19:03,920 --> 00:19:07,300
Today, we focused on type
checking and the optimizer.

259
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:13,020
But there's still more to
explore, like linker, and

260
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:18,700
LLVM itself. So take this diagram
as your map, and go on your

261
00:19:18,740 --> 00:19:20,740
own tour of the compiler.

262
00:19:20,780 --> 00:19:26,340
And if you want to dive deeper
into optimization, check out my

263
00:19:26,380 --> 00:19:30,860
session from NSSpain 2020, Swift
Optimizing at Compiler World.

264
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:39,920
Finally, let's talk about actions
we can take, now that we know all

265
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:43,540
of this. First, go deeper.

266
00:19:43,700 --> 00:19:45,060
The details are worth knowing.

267
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:50,760
Every new Swift feature has
a design reason behind it.

268
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:55,420
And what you learned today
helps you see those reasons.

269
00:19:55,460 --> 00:20:00,100
Use it as a lens, and you'll
enjoy the next feature even more.

270
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:03,460
Here is an example, some and any.

271
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:08,060
The some keyword shipped alongside
SwiftUI as Opaque Result Types.

272
00:20:08,120 --> 00:20:12,140
It was designed to overcome several
limitations of existential types.

273
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:18,220
behind these keywords lives the
Existential Container, a runtime mechanism.

274
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:23,760
So this is exactly where today's
knowledge becomes your lens.

275
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:28,380
If you want the full story,
check out this keyword

276
00:20:29,460 --> 00:20:35,104
and my session Deep Dive into any
and some, from SwiftHeroes 2023.

277
00:20:35,144 --> 00:20:39,300
And one more example: build artifact caching.

278
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:44,160
Swift library and frameworks and build
outputs can be cached effectively.

279
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:49,620
And many tools out there
take advantage of this,

280
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:51,120
like Tuist, and Scipio.

281
00:20:51,160 --> 00:20:56,040
Try exploring them, with the
knowledge you gained today.

282
00:20:57,460 --> 00:21:02,180
So, here's what I want to leave you:
understand Swift from the inside.

283
00:21:02,220 --> 00:21:07,020
Optimize through the lens of
the Runtime and the Compiler.

284
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:08,620
And, enjoy your Swift life!

285
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:11,340
Thank you so much for listening.

286
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:15,360
I know this session went a little
deep, but I hope you enjoyed

287
00:21:15,400 --> 00:21:20,120
the ride. The conference keeps going,
so please enjoy the sessions ahead!
